    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Problem\nWhen using an LSP that uses `textDocument/diagnostics` and you open a buffer, but the cursor is not on it when the LspNotify `textDocument/didOpen` method gets called, the file doesn't get diagnostics.\n\n### Example\nLets say you have installed the `eslint` LSP (which uses `textDocument/diagnostics`), the `nvim-tree` plugin (to open files) and `nvim-lspconfig` to configure the LSP.\nIf you use `nvim-tree` to preview a file (open file in a buffer and immediately switch back to the tree window), the file doesn't get diagnostics, even if you switch back to it after later. You would have to make text changes or reload to get them.\nThe issue is not tied to those specific plugins/lsp, they are just triggering it. You can trigger the problem in other ways too.\n\n### Source of the problem\nThe issue stems from the logic in `vim.lsp.diagnostic._enable(bufnr)` which gets called only for LSPs using the `textDocument/diagnostics` method. It creates a `LspNotify` autocmd for a `bufnr`, however it never gets called in the above example, because when the autocmd gets called its `bufnr` is the one of the current window (nvim-tree in the example) instead of the file we want to run diagnostics for (the bufnr in the `_enable` function). I'm assuming this is because `LspNotify` is scheduled in `Client:notify`.\n\n### Solution\nMaybe `LspAttach` can be used instead of `LspNotify` or `client` can be passed to `vim.lsp.diagnostic._enable` and use it in some way. I'll leave it to you to decide on the best solution since I don't know how other things might get affected by changes to the code.\n\n\n### Steps to reproduce using \"nvim --clean -u minimal_init.lua\"\n\nDescribing a `minimal_init.lua` would take a lot of time figuring it out a minimal setup that uses `netrw` (haven't used it before), creating a minimal `eslint` lsp config, etc. I don't want to spend time on this only to create an issue on github. Instead I can provide the reproduction steps:\n1. Create a `minimal_init.lua` which:\n1.1. Creates netrw mapping which opens a file in a split and instantly moves the focus back to netrw.\n1.2. Sets up minimal working eslint lsp config and enables it.\n2. Create a javascript file with some lint errors.\n3. Install the eslint LSP.\n4. Install eslint locally and create a basic config file for it (eslint.config.js).\n5. Confirm that the LSP is actually functioning when opening the js file.\n6. Open nvim, then netrw, then open the js file with the mapping which switches to netrw.\n7. Notice how diagnostics are not set even if you switch back to the js file.\n8. Notice that the `LspNotify` autocmd in `vim.lsp.diagnostic._enable` was never called and therefore `vim.lsp.diagnostic._refresh` was never called either.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nOpening a file should always provide diagnostics if enabled and available.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\n0.12.2\n\n### Language server name/version\n\neslint latest\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nNixOS 26.05\n\n### Log file\n\n_No response_",
