            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "So I looked a bit, and I can't seem to reproduce this. I tried both LuaLS and clangd and both immediately report diagnostics when I open a new file. (side note: the original issue in this PR was in fact one of the symptoms of the LspNotify notification _not_ having a buf that I fixed in https://github.com/neovim/neovim/commit/54188fa242582808fc1d0651786a4aa2f6295fa6)\n\nWhat I did notice was that diagnostics itself is enabled in the _set_defaults() function (like @ivangeorgiew mentioned), but it's the only lsp feature that is enabled there. The other defaults are for setting up mappings, buffer options, completion functions and the like. Other \"features\" (capabilities) are actually done even later, after LspAttach (users can disable features that are default-enabled or enable features that aren't default-enabled if they wish). However, most capabilities will trigger their initial request in the on_attach()/enable() function, which diagnostics does not do.\n\nSo, despite me being unable to reproduce this, it makes sense to do a few things here, I think:\n\n1. Call `_set_defaults()` before the _text_document_did_open_handler as @ivangeorgiew suggested\n2. Remove the diagnostic enable call from `_set_defaults()`\n3. Convert diagnostic module to a capability and enable it by default\n4. Add a request for diagnostics to the end of `_enable()` (or `on_attach()` if converted to a capability)",
